WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.470
- Thanks to CuriosityStream

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.040
for keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.430
By clicking the link in the description,

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.970
you'll also get Nebula for free,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.223
the streaming platform made
by and for creators like me.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.060
A platform guard rushes to give him

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.510
a helpful push from behind.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.690
That push ensures that he
makes it onto the train,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.230
but it also knocks the
box out of his arms.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.980
So, what's in the box?

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.814
What's in the box, Harry?

00:00:23.814 --> 00:00:27.120
(tense music)
(woman screams)

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.692
When are you responsible for an accident?

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:34.320
Imagine you're driving along
and you stop at a red light.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.370
A car then rear-ends you and sends you

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.840
flying into the car in front of you.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.293
Who's responsible?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.810
If you hadn't been there,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.140
there wouldn't have been an accident.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.950
But does that put you on
the hook, legally speaking?

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.060
If you knock down one domino,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.530
are you responsible for all
of the other dominoes falling?

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.720
When a butterfly flaps its wings,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.454
the effects echo outward
like ripples on a pond,

00:01:02.454 --> 00:01:05.663
and no one knows where
the effects will stop.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.120
At what point, though, do we say that

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.660
the butterfly caused the harm?

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.870
At what point do we say
that the downstream ripples

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.073
are too attenuated to
pin it on the butterfly?

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.850
When someone's sloppy
mistake puts your life

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.623
and limb at risk, who must pay?

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.883
What duty do we owe each other?

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.146
That's the question that we must answer in

00:01:34.146 --> 00:01:37.180
The Case of Poor Mrs. Palsgraf,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.030
she never saw it coming.
(train horn honks)

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.480
Submitted for your adjudication,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.240
our story begins innocently enough,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.540
with a family trip to the beach.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.540
The year is 1924, the
place, Brooklyn, New York.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.170
On a hot and muggy August morning,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.780
Helen Palsgraf prepares
to take her two daughters

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.830
on a long-awaited trip to Rockaway Beach.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.110
The girls have been on
their very best behavior

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.530
for weeks, helping around the house,

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.450
working tirelessly at their jobs

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.350
in the nearby clothing factory.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.310
They diligently counted
down the days to today.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.820
They earned this break
from the tiresome monotony

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.230
of the daily grind.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.350
Mrs. Palsgraf too worked hard
to have this rare day off.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.690
She works two full-time jobs
doing back-breaking work

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.760
both as a housekeeper and as a janitor.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.520
She rarely sleeps, instead
painstakingly saving

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.480
every penny earned to
scrape just enough together

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.720
to be able to afford the train fare

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.890
and take the girls out for one summer day.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.030
It was meant to be a respite,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.778
a breath of fresh air in every sense.

00:02:44.778 --> 00:02:47.050
(thunder cracks)

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.613
But fate had other plans.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.110
Having meticulously packed their bags,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.340
the Palsgraf family slowly makes the trek

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.970
over to the Atlantic Avenue train station.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:01.000
At the ticket booth, Mrs.
Palsgraf pauses briefly,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.560
considering the expense of the tickets.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.570
She looks down at her children's filthy

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.650
but beaming faces, their
smiles are well worth it.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.040
She dutifully hands over
her hard-earned wages

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.040
and purchases three tickets
on the Long Island Railroad.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.150
Destination, Rockaway Beach.
(cash register dings)

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.010
The women make their way
down to the train platform.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.750
As they patiently stand
in the sweltering heat

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:27.910
waiting for their train to arrive,

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.256
they daydream about the sand, the surf,

00:03:30.256 --> 00:03:32.500
and the fresh air that await them.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.140
Little did they know,
(dramatic music)

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.555
they would never reach their destination.

00:03:37.555 --> 00:03:38.680
(screaming)

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.180
Meanwhile, several blocks away,

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.100
a man is abruptly awoken
by the thump, thump, thump

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.900
of his landlord's fist
pounding on his door.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.210
We'll call this man Hot Mess Harry.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.650
Harry starts this morning
like most mornings,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:56.400
running two steps
behind, he has overslept.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.310
He is three months late on the rent

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.740
and knows that his landlord
is inching ever-closer

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.700
to fulfilling his frequent promise

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.570
to break his legs if he doesn't
come up with the money soon,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.490
and just in case he has
momentarily forgotten,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.350
the landlord reiterates the point,

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.700
barking through the door to come out

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.510
and that he knows he's in there.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.180
Harry scoops up his rumpled clothes

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.960
from off the floor and throws them on

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.930
as quickly and quietly as possible.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.210
He hops around the room,
shoving his feet into his shoes

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.050
while trying to simultaneously
button his shirt.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.140
He turns, raises the bedroom window,

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.520
and prepares to sneak
out down the fire escape.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.980
But he pauses and doubles
back to grab a large box

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.040
before slinking out through the back.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.700
Box in hand, Harry now
tears down the street

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.010
and into the train station,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.624
leaving a path of chaos and
destruction in his wake.

00:04:39.624 --> 00:04:40.457
(man yells)

00:04:40.457 --> 00:04:43.433
Come hell or high water, he
has to make it to the train.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.930
But Harry pauses for a moment
at the bottom of the stairs

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.390
leading up to the train platform, panting.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.120
He swears to himself,
wishing that he weren't being

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.564
slowed down by the weight
of this inconvenient parcel.

00:04:55.564 --> 00:04:59.500
It's heavy, but he's got to
get rid of that damn box.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.180
Just then, he catches
a glimpse of the train.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.480
It's already here, he picks up the pace,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.940
throwing caution and innocent
bystanders into the wind

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.633
as he sprints up the stairs.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.480
The train crew is making
their final preparations

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.010
to leave the the station,
this is gonna be a close one.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.250
He reaches for the train just
as the doors begin closing.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.200
The crew pity him, but time
and the Long Island Railroad

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.960
wait for no man.
(man yells)

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.780
Harry makes a desperate leap
onto the now-moving train.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.460
A platform guard rushes to give him

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.910
a helpful push from behind.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.070
That push ensures that he
makes it onto the train,

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.630
but it also knocks the
box out of his arms.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.410
So, what's in the box?

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.913
What's in the box, Harry?

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:43.850
Fireworks, Legal Eagles, the
box is chock full of fireworks,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.550
and what happens next
depends on who you ask.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.840
Scenario A, in the first scenario,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.700
the train employee's push knocks the box

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.810
out of Harry's arms and into the air.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.730
For a moment, it seems to
float, suspended in midair,

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:01.000
but then it begins its
inevitable descent downward,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.640
taking an unlucky bounce off
the very edge of the platform.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.650
It ricochets off and bounces again,

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.470
this time off the side of the train itself

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.640
and then continues bobbling its way down

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.550
onto the train track cavity itself,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.750
where it eventually settles into its final

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.760
resting place, lodged
between the two track beams,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.180
inches from the train wheels,

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.360
which have begun to
move out of the station.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.530
At this point, it's worth
noting a piece of metal

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.480
on the undercarriage
of the train's caboose,

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.100
which loosened over the
years of wear and tear,

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.420
juts out ever so slightly to one side,

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.520
perfectly aligned on a
collision course with the box.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.810
This metal occasionally
scrapes against the tracks,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.430
creating a burst of screeching
sparks when it does,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:42.280
and just as the caboose
passes near the box,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.300
the metal meets the track again.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.140
The ensuing sparks set off
just one firework at first.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.620
(firework booms)

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.007
Then another, until like popcorn,

00:06:51.007 --> 00:06:54.280
all of the fireworks go
off in rapid succession.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.200
The explosion causes a stampede

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.350
as panicked passengers push and shove

00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.910
their way towards the exits.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.600
Luggage is left behind, knocked over,

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.120
burst open, and trampled on.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.690
Undershirts, nightgowns, toiletries

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.060
are strewn about the floor.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.960
People shout, trying to
find their loved ones

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.630
in the thick, smoky fog.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.760
In the middle of it all,

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:13.800
Mrs. Palsgraf blindly
searches for her way out.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.690
She cannot see the man fumbling past her,

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:19.050
who himself unable to see,
knocks into an enormous

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:20.920
floor scale at just the right angle

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.240
to cause the scale to topple over,

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.240
and it comes down right on
top of poor Mrs. Palsgraf.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:32.220
That's right, folks, an
industrial, heavy brass,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.030
guess-your-weight old-timey scale,

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.060
though not so old-timey for Mrs. Palsgraf.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.664
The scale is coin-operated
and those coins now begin to

00:07:39.664 --> 00:07:43.028
slowly trickle out, plunking
her right on top of her head

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:47.580
as it slowly crushes her with
all of its mighty weight.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.310
Or the second scenario,
maybe it happened like this.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.900
The train employee's push knocks the box

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.480
out of Harry's arms.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.870
The box, a veritable powder
keg immediately explodes

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:00.050
upon impact with the ground.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:02.480
The force of the explosion is so powerful

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.927
that it knocks the scale
next to Mrs. Palsgraf over

00:08:04.927 --> 00:08:08.190
and it falls right on top of
her with its mighty weight.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:11.800
Either way, these particular
facts are unimportant.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.980
The point is that Mrs.
Palsgraf is badly hurt.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:16.550
Her daughters rush to her aid.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:19.130
Still pinned under the
crushing weight of the scale,

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.640
she meekly tells them
that she's very sorry,

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:23.360
but she doesn't think she'll be able

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.210
to join them at the beach today.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.190
She's in shock and denial,

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.270
unable to yet comprehend that her life

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.470
has been irrevocably changed.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.420
She suffers bruises all over her body

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.490
and experiences chronic headaches.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.993
Doctors will later diagnose
her with traumatic hysteria.

00:08:38.993 --> 00:08:41.817
She develops a stammer
and becomes depressed.

00:08:41.817 --> 00:08:44.630
She is no longer able to work

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:47.230
and must give up both of her jobs.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.763
She requires expensive medicines
and round-the-clock care.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:55.240
Her bones eventually heal,

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.041
but Mrs. Palsgraf is never quite the same.

00:08:58.041 --> 00:09:00.730
When she is lucid enough
to recount the events

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.530
of that fateful August day,

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.197
and helplessly watch the
medical bills pile up,

00:09:05.197 --> 00:09:09.550
she is above all else, angry.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.790
But this is America,
and someone has to pay

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:15.320
for what happened to her.
(cash register dings)

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:17.047
But what about Hot Mess Harry?

00:09:17.047 --> 00:09:19.030
While clearly he's at fault here

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.188
and should be liable for the damages,

00:09:21.188 --> 00:09:24.150
that's not what this story is about.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.800
Harry never paid Mrs. Palsgraf
a dime for two reasons.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.000
One, he didn't have any money,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.493
and two, he was never
seen or heard from again.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.630
And even if Mrs. Palsgraf
were able to track him down

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.680
and he were served with a lawsuit,

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.940
he would have been useless to her.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.430
He's what we call judgment-proof,

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.060
meaning that he wouldn't
have had the funds

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.260
to cover a judgment
against him in any case.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.370
This is often a sad
reality about litigation,

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.624
and with nowhere else to turn,

00:09:54.624 --> 00:09:58.763
Mrs. Palsgraf sues the Long
Island Railroad Company.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.500
She claims that the railroad was negligent

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.030
when its employee pushed
Harry onto the train,

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.350
and although Harry is primarily at fault,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.230
she was also harmed by the negligence

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.040
of the railroad company.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.960
At trial, a jury awards
her $6,000 in damages,

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:17.750
around $900,000 by today's standards,

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.930
but the railroad appeals.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.538
Mrs. Palsgraf wins again.

00:10:22.538 --> 00:10:25.330
And finally, the case makes
its way all the way up

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.680
to New York's highest
court, the court of appeals.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.510
The issue on the table,

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.150
was the Long Island
Railroad Company negligent

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.270
towards Mrs. Palsgraf?

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:36.810
Could they have foreseen that someone

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.003
would bring fireworks on the train?

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:44.423
In other words, what
exactly is negligence?

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.350
We now turn to Albany,

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:50.408
where courtroom proceedings
are just getting underway.

00:10:50.408 --> 00:10:54.850
(typewriter clicks)
(tense music)

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.810
- [Judge] This court has had
the opportunity to review

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.860
the record, are counselors
really for oral argument?

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:02.140
- [William] Yes, your Honor,
William McNamara representing

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:04.680
the appellants, the Long
Island Railroad Company.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.770
- [Matthew] Matthew Wood, sir,

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.810
representing the appellee,
Mrs. Helen Palsgraf.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.150
- [Judge] Okay then
gentlemen, let's get started.

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.050
- [William] Thank you and
may it please the court,

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.690
your Honors, my client,
the Long Island Railroad,

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.930
has been accused of negligence
toward Helen Palsgraf

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.080
when one of its employees helped
a passenger onto his train

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.870
by giving him a supportive nudge aboard.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.020
There was no negligence here.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.790
How could we possibly expect that employee

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.360
to know that the box
in the passenger's arms

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.280
was packed with fireworks?

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.950
There was no signage or any
other sort of indication

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.470
that the box contained dangerous contents.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.040
There was simply no way
for a reasonable person

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.970
to know that if this
particular box were dropped,

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.230
it would explode; my
client was not negligent.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:51.500
On the contrary, the employee
was doing the right thing.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:53.110
Don't we want to live in a society

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.550
in which people feel
able to help each other,

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.720
rather than second guess
every move they make

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:59.550
in fear of future liability?

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.120
The passenger is the
only one to blame here.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:02.953
- [Matthew] Your Honors,

00:12:02.953 --> 00:12:05.310
the railroad was clearly negligent here.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:08.280
The employee pushed the man
onto the train far too hard.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:11.850
His carelessness forced the
passenger to drop the box,

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.220
which directly caused the explosion

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.920
that has devastated poor Mrs. Palsgraf.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.720
If not for the railroad's actions,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.360
Mrs. Palsgraf would not have been injured.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.130
It's a logical certainty.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:24.990
- [Judge] Mr. McNamara, let's assume that

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.440
the railroad employee was
in fact negligent here

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.150
with regard to the passenger.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:32.040
Would Mrs. Palsgraf be
entitled to damages?

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.000
- [William] Your Honors, even if we accept

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.520
that there could have been negligence

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.470
in how the employee helped
the passenger board the train,

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.970
that remains a matter exclusively between

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.670
the passenger and my client.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.900
Mrs. Palsgraf just has
nothing to do with it.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.030
- [Matthew] Your Honors, my client

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:48.710
has everything to do with it.

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.300
She did everything right
that fateful August day.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.810
She was a paying customer
on the Long Island Railroad

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.170
who obeyed all the rules,
minded her business,

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:59.050
and waited exactly where
the railroad told her.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:00.850
- [Judge] And what
exactly would you suggest

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.840
that the railroad owes to
paying customers, counselor?

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.750
- [Matthew] The railroad
owes Mrs. Palsgraf

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:08.730
and all of its customers a duty of care,

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.420
and it breached that duty
when its employee's negligence

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:13.270
created an unsafe environment,

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.600
causing my client's harrowing injury.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.800
If an injury can be traced
back to a wrongful act

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.460
and there are no intervening events,

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.660
that is sufficient to establish liability.

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:25.620
- [William] But if we hold
the railroad negligent

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.050
to Mrs. Palsgraf, where
do we draw the line?

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:30.700
We simply cannot hold
my client responsible

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:32.780
for the consequences of all actions,

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.100
no matter how extraordinary or bizarre,

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.360
that impact customers of the railroad.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.930
The duty of care must be limited to those

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:43.330
who are reasonably foreseeable
under the circumstances.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:44.640
- [Judge] Counselor, don't you think that

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.040
Mrs. Palsgraf's injuries
were reasonably foreseeable?

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:49.030
- [William] Though regrettable,

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.430
her injures were not foreseeable.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.680
Who could know that the box
would explode upon falling,

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.400
and that that explosion
would knock over a scale

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.300
all the way across the platform,

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:02.620
and that scale would somehow
topple on top of her?

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:05.080
The chain of events here is too tenuous.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.690
- [Matthew] But my client
was foreseeable, your Honor,

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:10.300
she was in the zone of foreseeable danger.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.130
Mrs. Palsgraf's close physical proximity

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:15.330
to the careless act matters here.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.700
She was waiting on the designated platform

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.070
for her imminent train,

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:21.520
about 10 feet from the
site of the explosion.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.760
She was clearly at risk
of direct physical impact.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:27.020
- [Judge] Mr. McNamara,
what do you say to that?

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:29.280
- [William] Even if we accept
the zone of danger test,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.440
Mrs. Palsgraf was still too
far away from the explosion

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.720
to have a successful negligence
claim under that standard.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:38.600
It is definitely not
reasonably foreseeable

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.570
that a scale all the way on
the other end of the station

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.380
could fall on top of her as a result

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.420
of one employee's careless push.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:47.320
There are just too many variables

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.270
in between the two occurrences.

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.624
- [Judge] Thank you, gentlemen,
this matter is adjourned.

00:14:51.624 --> 00:14:54.518
(typewriter clicks)

00:14:54.518 --> 00:14:56.000
(tense music)

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.970
- Chief judge and future
Supreme Court Justice Cardozo

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.990
wrote the decision of the
New York court of appeals

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.700
and what is considered by
many to be the single most

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.737
influential torts case in all
of American jurisprudence.

00:15:06.737 --> 00:15:08.440
Do you have what it takes to think

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.610
like a future Supreme Court justice?

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.750
Pause this video, put on
your favorite black robe,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:14.820
and grab your gavel and
let me know in the comments

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:18.545
how you think this case
was decided, I'll wait.

00:15:18.545 --> 00:15:20.960
(typewriter clicks)
(tense music)

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.490
Cardozo sided with the
railroad company and found that

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.330
they did not have a duty
of care to Mrs. Palsgraf.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.630
Her injuries were not
reasonably foreseeable

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:31.700
because the explosive box
didn't appear to be dangerous

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:34.100
and a reasonable person
would not have foreseen

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:36.560
that a scale would topple
on top of Mrs. Palsgraf

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.710
as a result of the box
falling and exploding.

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:41.783
Not only was her judgment reversed,

00:15:41.783 --> 00:15:44.260
but Mrs. Palsgraf actually ended up owing

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:47.175
the Long Island Railroad Company $560

00:15:47.175 --> 00:15:49.600
to cover their attorney's fees,

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.936
the equivalent of a
year's worth of her wages.

00:15:52.936 --> 00:15:55.100
This case is probably the most famous

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.140
American tort case in
all of jurisprudence.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.910
Its legacy, however, has eroded over time.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:02.450
The dissent in this case found that

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.920
because there was little
to no gap in time or space

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.290
between the train employee's negligence

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:09.950
and Mrs. Palsgraf's
injuries, the injuries were

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:12.930
a natural consequence of the
series of events that followed.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.640
And while some states still
follow Cardozo's approach

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.800
and find no liability for
an unforeseeable plaintiff,

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.710
most states now agree with the dissent

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:22.820
and focus on the proximity
in time and space

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:24.960
to a negligence in a plaintiff's injuries.

